# Always commit and push

After finishing work that changes files, commit and push in the same turn. Do not wait for the user to ask.

## Do

- Stage the files you changed (and any new files that belong in the repo).
- Commit with a concise message that says why the change exists.
- Push the current branch to `origin` with a regular `git push` (use `-u` if the branch has no upstream).
- If a pre-commit hook edits files, make a new commit with those fixes. Do not amend a commit that was already pushed.

## Do not

- Do not leave finished work uncommitted.
- Do not commit `.env`, `.env.local`, tokens, keys, or credential files.
- Do not force-push, skip hooks, or rewrite history unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not push to `main`/`master` with `--force`.

## Example

```text
# ✅ After adding a feature
git add src/app/page.tsx src/components/coo-checklist.tsx
git commit -m "Add COO checklist split on Home so Joseph can run today/week/month from one board."
git push

# ❌ Leave the working tree dirty and only describe the change
```
